      "obligation": "refuter:3:1",
      "id": "prop-the-radon-nikodym-derivative-is-integrable-exactly-when-the-absolutely-continuous-part-is-finite",
      "location": "Proof step 1.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 applies [L2] to conclude |ν_a|(X)=∫|dν_a/dμ|dμ before either side of the claimed equivalence has established that ν_a is finite. But [L2] explicitly says: “For an absolutely continuous finite signed or finite complex measure, the total variation has density …”. The Statement permits a nonfinite signed ν_a; for example ν_a=λ relative to λ on R satisfies the decomposition hypotheses but is not finite. Thus [L2] does not license step 1.1 in the case needed to prove the reverse implication.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "6783eff192096b22556115e184775954ded8f4e6d5700903d7902ca3a670e9ef"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:3:2",
      "id": "rem-von-neumanns-hilbert-space-proof-of-radon-nikodym",
      "location": "Remark",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The Remark says “the functional g↦∫g dν is bounded on L²(μ) when ν≪μ and finite”. Take μ=λ on (0,1) and dν=x^{-1/2}dλ. Then ν is finite and ν≪μ, but h=x^{-1/2} is not in L²(μ). With g_n=h·1_{[1/n,1]}/||h·1_{[1/n,1]}||₂, one has ||g_n||₂=1 while ∫g_n dν=||h·1_{[1/n,1]}||₂→∞. Hence this functional is not bounded on L²(μ).",
